// SMR: a one-to-one recreation of the 4ms Spectral Multiband Resonator firmware DSP,
// (C) 2015 Dan Green and Hugo Paris, MIT licence (https://github.com/4ms/SMR).
// Filter algorithm after Max Mathews and Julius O. Smith III, "Methods for Synthesizing
// Very High Q Parametrically Well Behaved Two Pole Filters".
// Six resonant bandpass channels, each tuned to one of 20 notes of a scale (11 scales in
// each of 19 banks); odd channels filter the left input into the left output, even ones the
// right. SPREAD fans the channels out around the centre channel, ROTATE walks every channel
// one note up or down the scale per tempo division (SCALE ROTATE carries the walk into the
// next scale when it wraps), ROOT jumps all channels like the ROTATE CV jack, SCALE like the
// SCALE CV jack, and MORPH sets how fast each channel crossfades from one note to the next.
// FILTER picks the firmware's 2-pass (two cascaded Mathews-Smith resonators crossfaded by Q),
// 1-pass MaxQ, or BpRe (two-pole bandpass blended between Q 2 and Q 800 coefficient sets).
// The firmware's scale tables were converted from 96 kHz coefficients to note frequencies,
// and the coefficients are recomputed for 44.1 kHz: frequency from 2 sin(pi f/fs), decay
// and resonance gain matched to the 96 kHz filters; its exp/log/two-pass calibration tables
// are kept (decimated). Omitted: LEDs, flash, user scales, channel locks, frequency blocks,
// sliders and level CVs (all channels at full level, so CV lag does nothing), envelope and
// V/oct outputs, the frequency nudge knob and 1V/oct jacks (replaced by TRANSPOSE, which in
// BpRe shifts the frequency rather than interpolating towards the next note). The motion
// update runs every 8 samples instead of on its own 20 kHz timer; at most two rotate steps
// queue while a morph is still running.
